INSERT INTO "articles" ("id","slug","title","dek","author","body","status","read_time","created_at","updated_at","published_at","layout_style","tasks_json","header_image_key","header_image_alt","stage","topic","attachments_json","subgroup") VALUES (214,'the-great-gatsby-glut','The ‘Great Gatsby’ Glut','F. Scott Fitzgerald’s classic novel about America and aspiration is now in the public domain, so new editions, as well as a graphic novel and a zombie adaptation, have gotten the green light.','Ian','I’ve long held the completely unsupported notion that a protagonist is simpler to write than a truly memorable supporting character. Sometimes just a silhouette — created with a few strokes of the pen, a few evocative adjectives — seems the more unlikely accomplishment, born of some surplus wit and energy, some surfeit of love for a fictional world that expresses itself in the desire to animate even its most minor participants. F. Scott Fitzgerald excelled at this sort of character. Few can write a more vivid neighbor, train conductor, or, more usually, bartender. Take Owl Eyes (or so he’s called, for his large spectacles), one of the many partygoers at Gatsby’s mansion. When we first meet him, he wanders into the library and doesn’t seem able to escape — he stands paralyzed, staring at the books in inebriated admiration. 

I wonder if we’re all Owl Eyes now. In the century or so since *The Great Gatsby* was published, we have been lost in Gatsby’s house, immured in a never-ending revival. This revival will only get more crowded now that the novel’s copyright has expired. January 2021 saw the publication of a new edition from Modern Library, with an introduction by Wesley Morris, a critic at large at *The New York Times*, and another from Penguin, introduced by the novelist Min Jin Lee. That month also brought a prequel, *Nick*, by Michael Farris Smith.

All this follows several films, theater adaptations, and other retellings. The novel has been transplanted to post-9/11 Manhattan in Joseph O’Neill’s *Netherland*, to 21st-century London (our bootlegger becomes a Russian arms-dealing billionaire) in Vesna Goldsworthy’s *Gorsky*, to the home of a Black family in contemporary North Carolina in Stephanie Powell Watts’s *No One Is Coming to Save Us*. Gatsby has inspired immersive theater, young-adult novels, and a Taylor Swift song — “Happiness” — that weaves together lines and images from the novel. Even the most minor characters have had spinoffs — Pammie, age 3 in Fitzgerald’s book, gets her own story in *Daisy Buchanan’s Daughter* by Tom Carson. (Apparently she becomes LBJ’s confidante in Carson’s work, and honestly, why not at this point?) All this atop a heap of Fitzgeraldiana, new biographies, and scholarship — to say nothing of the humming cottage industry dedicated to Zelda Fitzgerald, newly resurrected as a feminist heroine. The literary term for this profusion of interpretations born of a novel’s wide influence and deep purchase on the imagination is a sheer glut. 

Why doesn’t it irritate me more? Perhaps it’s because the book occupies such a peculiar place in the culture. Is there a major novel so established in the canon and curriculum whose literary merit and moral probity remain so regularly and passionately contested? We’re not speaking of books like *Huckleberry Finn*, mired in a confused, perpetual debate about racist language and censorship. With *The Great Gatsby*, the question is simpler and stranger: Can Fitzgerald write? Is the book a masterpiece — what T. S. Eliot called “the first step that American fiction has taken since Henry James” — or, as Gore Vidal would put it, the work of a writer who was “barely literate”? 

The novel has become subject to all its own barbs; every one of Fitzgerald’s bitter observations is lobbed back at his book in turn. As Nick wonders of Gatsby, so readers have wondered of the novel: Is this shallowness I perceive, or miraculous depth? Like Daisy, the book is derided as pretty and meretricious. Like Nick, it is accused of being passive, or worse — complicit in the spectacle it appears to criticize. Even admirers have their own debates: The book is good, but great? Great — but not the greatness of assurance and cut-glass perfection. It’s the greatness of a vastly open, unstable, slithery text. Within the scaffolding of its tidy, three-act structure and its carefully patterned symmetries, sprouts an unruly blend of stiff moralism and wild ambivalence, its infatuation with and contempt for wealth, its empathy alongside its desire to punish its characters.

Fitzgerald was proud of what he had achieved. “I think my novel is about the best American novel ever written,” he crowed. The book baffled reviewers, however, and sold poorly. “Of all the reviews, even the most enthusiastic, not one had the slightest idea what the book was about,” Fitzgerald wrote to the critic Edmund Wilson. That matter remains unsettled. The book has been treated as a beautiful bauble, fundamentally unserious. In a 1984 essay in *The Times*, John Kenneth Galbraith sniffed that Fitzgerald was only superficially interested in class. “It is the lives of the rich — their enjoyments, agonies, and putative insanity — that attract his interest,” he wrote. “Their social and political consequences escape him as he himself escaped such matters in his own life.”

This interpretation has been turned on its head. Both new editions make light of the book’s beauty — it’s the treatment of the grotesque that is so compelling (Morris compares the characters to the “Real Housewives”). Both make the case that the book’s value lies in its critique of capitalism. Lee describes Fitzgerald as “a fan of Karl Marx,” and writes that *The Great Gatsby* remains “a modern novel by exploring the intersection of social hierarchy, white femininity, white male love, and unfettered capitalism.” For Morris, too, there is no romance between Gatsby and Daisy but “capitalism as an emotion”: “Gatsby meets Daisy when he’s a broke soldier, senses that she requires more prosperity, so five years later he returns as almost a parody of it. So the tragedy here is the death of the heart.” 

The evidence exists, in Fitzgerald’s complicated way, as we look at the text and the biography. He was riven by bitterness toward the rich, yet, as a child, pretended to be the foundling son of a king, falling for Zelda because she looked expensive. So much waffling, according to critics who want less equivocation and stronger moral stances, while others crave subtlety. As we’re borne back into this text, courting endless interpretation might just be the safer bet for immortality — after all, there’s great honor in being a supporting character.','published',7,'2026-09-04T20:03:41.301Z','2026-09-05T17:38:55.516Z','2026-09-04T20:03:41.301Z','poster','[{"id":"paragraph-match-1788629448297","type":"regional-stage","title":"Questions 26–40","instructions":"In which part of the text is the following mentioned?","questions":[{"number":26,"prompt":"the connection between personal relationships and economic forces","answers":["G"]},{"number":27,"prompt":"a work’s simultaneous admiration and condemnation","answers":["E"]},{"number":28,"prompt":"the transformation of a novel into a wider cultural phenomenon","answers":["C"]},{"number":29,"prompt":"a wealth of adaptations inspired by one novel","answers":["C"]},{"number":30,"prompt":"the idea that endless reinterpretation may ensure a work’s survival","answers":["H"]},{"number":31,"prompt":"doubts about an acclaimed author’s true ability","answers":["D"]},{"number":32,"prompt":"the author’s confidence being undermined by early public reception","answers":["F"]},{"number":33,"prompt":"greater skill required to create memorable secondary characters","answers":["A"]},{"number":34,"prompt":"a classic text whose meaning remains open to multiple interpretations","answers":["E"]},{"number":35,"prompt":"a later work expanding the story through an alternative perspective","answers":["B"]},{"number":36,"prompt":"a surge of interest following the expiry of copyright","answers":["B"]},{"number":37,"prompt":"the difficulty of reconciling an author’s personal beliefs with his own desires","answers":["H"]},{"number":38,"prompt":"the view that the writer failed to address wider social issues","answers":["F"]},{"number":39,"prompt":"the ability of minor details to create a vivid fictional world","answers":["A"]},{"number":40,"prompt":"a shift towards viewing the novel as a social critique","answers":["G"]}]}]','66a64858-cb1a-4bd4-b459-3782261ffdf5.webp','','regional','Reading','[]',NULL);
INSERT INTO "articles" ("id","slug","title","dek","author","body","status","read_time","created_at","updated_at","published_at","layout_style","tasks_json","header_image_key","header_image_alt","stage","topic","attachments_json","subgroup") VALUES (649,'art-beyond-humanity','Art Beyond Humanity','Traditional definitions restrict art to humans, but some creatures challenge this boundary. Whether beauty comes from a paintbrush, a bird, or a glacier, the awe it inspires remains the same.','Ian','In my lifetime, I’ve encountered some genuinely peculiar things masquerading as art. I vividly recall Damien Hirst’s *Sensation* exhibition, with its sharks and cows sawn apart, provoking as much bewilderment as admiration. Art and beauty, it seems, lie firmly in the eye of the beholder. What I deem art, another might dismiss as rubbish—and, in some cases, it might literally be rubbish.

Anthropologists broadly define art as a uniquely human activity, one in which individuals create objects or performances for the aesthetic appreciation of others. Yet human motives rarely align so neatly with this idealistic view. Modern artists are acutely aware of the commercial potential of their work and the additional benefits that success might bring, such as fame and admiration. It’s hard to imagine that art, even in its earliest forms, was ever purely about aesthetic appreciation.

This anthropological framework also implies that animals cannot be artists. Consider the male bird-of-paradise, whose dazzling plumage might strike us as beautiful, or birdsong, which is often likened to music. Displays of animal behaviour are sometimes described as dance, but do these activities meet the criteria for art? The answer hinges on intention. While birdsong can improve with practice, it is largely innate rather than consciously composed. The same applies to elaborate behaviours such as animal ‘dances’ or architectural feats like the intricately woven nests of weaverbirds. These nests, though impressive, are evolutionary adaptations designed to house eggs, not examples of artistic self-expression. While it is tempting to attribute artistic qualities to animal behaviour, closer inspection often reveals it to be instinct-driven or environmentally shaped. My voice might sound pleasing to some, but its qualities are not the product of artistic intent.

However, a few cases in the animal kingdom challenge this division, suggesting that some creatures might, in fact, be considered artists under human definitions. The male lyrebird of central Australia provides a fascinating example. Unlike most birds, lyrebirds do not inherit their songs from their fathers. Instead, they sample sounds from their surroundings, weaving them into complex, original compositions. This process resembles the work of a DJ, who combines snippets of existing music into something new. These performances aim to attract females, a motive not dissimilar to that of many human musicians. Captive lyrebirds have even been observed mimicking chainsaws and other mechanical sounds and incorporating them into their repertoire. By creating non-innate music for the appreciation of others—female lyrebirds, in this case—the lyrebird might fulfil the anthropological criteria for art. Nevertheless, sceptics have argued that most birdsong fails to qualify as music because it does not conform to human musical scales. Yet this seems an overly rigid view, given the vast range of musical scales humans themselves have developed. What one person hears as noise, another might perceive as music.

Another intriguing example of possible animal artistry is the male bowerbird. These birds create and decorate intricate twig sculptures to attract mates. The males enhance their displays by using visual illusions and arranging objects in ways that manipulate perspective, much like the artist M. C. Escher. Placing larger objects farther away creates a forced perspective that exaggerates their size and impresses potential mates. If a female deems the display sufficiently artistic, she will mate with the male, who, it is theorised, signals his genetic fitness through his creative prowess. Interestingly, once the mating ritual concludes, the female departs to build her own nest and raise her offspring alone.

Captive animals, such as cats and chimpanzees, have also been observed producing what could loosely be called art. When given paint and brushes, zoo-housed chimpanzees sometimes create works reminiscent of Jackson Pollock’s abstract pieces. However, these activities are human-induced rather than spontaneous, raising doubts about whether they can be considered genuine expressions of artistic intent. One exception might be Washoe, the famous sign-language-using chimpanzee, who reportedly described her paintings in symbolic terms, hinting at a degree of self-awareness.

The lyrebird, the bowerbird, and Washoe the chimpanzee are just a handful of examples of animals whose behaviours could be viewed as art through the lens of human definitions. The natural world likely harbours many more undiscovered artists. Yet this raises a broader question: is it necessary to distinguish between what is beautiful and what is artistic? Perhaps what truly matters is the capacity for aesthetic appreciation, regardless of whether the object in question is a glacier-sculpted mountain, a bowerbird’s twig arrangement, or Michelangelo’s *David*. All three evoke a similar emotional response—one of awe and wonder. Personally, I see no need to categorise these experiences. Beauty, whether shaped by nature, animals, or human hands, has the power to move us, and that, surely, is enough.','published',6,'2026-09-05T08:39:55.852Z','2026-09-05T17:49:30.872Z','2026-09-05T11:01:32.121Z','poster','[{"id":"regional-stage-1788642204058","type":"regional-stage","title":"Questions 26–40","instructions":"Read the text and answer questions 26–40 below. In which part of the text is the following mentioned?","questions":[{"number":26,"prompt":"rewards that may lie behind creative activity","answers":["B"]},{"number":27,"prompt":"emotional response taking precedence over classification","answers":["G"]},{"number":28,"prompt":"existing material being reworked into something new","answers":["D"]},{"number":29,"prompt":"creative skill conferring biological advantages","answers":["E"]},{"number":30,"prompt":"widely differing views about artistic merit","answers":["A"]},{"number":31,"prompt":"criticism of applying overly restrictive criteria to art","answers":["D"]},{"number":32,"prompt":"evidence of cognizance behind a creative act","answers":["F"]},{"number":33,"prompt":"success in courtship bringing no lasting partnership","answers":["E"]},{"number":34,"prompt":"impressive work serving a practical purpose","answers":["C"]},{"number":35,"prompt":"doubts over the value of drawing firm artistic boundaries","answers":["G"]},{"number":36,"prompt":"those on stage may likewise be seeking romantic attention","answers":["D"]},{"number":37,"prompt":"uncertainty over creativity produced under artificial conditions","answers":["F"]},{"number":38,"prompt":"a factor separating artistry from instinctive behaviour","answers":["C"]},{"number":39,"prompt":"the likelihood of unrecognized creativity elsewhere in nature","answers":["G"]},{"number":40,"prompt":"deliberate manipulation of visual perception","answers":["E"]}]}]','64e76fbc-cc9f-4733-83c4-40d3ced680df.jpg','','regional','Reading','[]',NULL);
INSERT INTO "articles" ("id","slug","title","dek","author","body","status","read_time","created_at","updated_at","published_at","layout_style","tasks_json","header_image_key","header_image_alt","stage","topic","attachments_json","subgroup") VALUES (650,'why-animals-like-to-play','Why Animals Like to Play','From dogs to ravens and herring gulls, animals exhibit an extraordinary desire to play. John Guiver looks into their behaviour.','Ian','‘Play’ has been described as ‘any form of behavior that does not serve a function or purpose’. The natural world abounds with such examples of animals playing just because they can. For instance, ravens in Alaska and Northern Canada have been observed sliding down roofs covered in snow. They slide down and then fly or walk back to the top of the roof to repeat the activity again and again. In some parts of America, ravens have been spotted sliding down shallow banks of snow, often with a small stick held in their claws. When I learned this, it immediately brought to mind when my children were young and I took them to the park. They exhibited exactly the same **26) …** behavior, happy to do the same thing for hour upon hour on the slides.

Understanding why and how animals or birds play requires spending hours watching them. They help us to explain what is often a mystery to humans – are they really playing, or is it just part of their normal instinct to survive? Herring gulls are another example of birds that seem to play. Shellfish like clams make up their staple diet, but they need to be opened to be eaten. To get inside the shell, herring gulls have to drop the clam onto a rock or another solid object. If they drop it from the right height, the shell covering the clam will crack or break, giving them access to the clam inside. But sometimes, instead of letting the shell hit the ground, the herring gulls swoop down and catch them in mid-air. Other gulls have been seen to behave like this, too, so the herring gulls’ activities are not unique.

This is where systematic research is so important. More than eighty hours of observing herring gulls has led scholars to discover patterns – or what they term ‘rules’ of the ‘game’. They found that it is the younger, less mature gulls that play this game the most. They also noticed that it is frequently played over soft ground rather than hard, so they are not actually trying to break the shells. The young herring gulls also played with objects other than clam shells. Interestingly, the scholars realized that this dropping behavior was seen more often when there was a strong wind. The scholars concluded that the gulls enjoyed the game more when there was an added challenge, in this case, the wind. What scholars are still uncertain about is if the gulls are simply having fun or if there is another reason for their behavior.

Both the ravens and the herring gulls play alone, but social play is common in animals, especially in dogs. Dog owners love to take their canine friend for a walk, yet when they meet another dog, the dogs go through a ritual just like it was stage-managed. Nose to nose, they look each other over, sniff one another, and circle around the other dog. Then, more likely than not, they will start to fight. Neither dog gets hurt because this is just a pretend fight, not the real thing. In other words, they are just play-fighting.

This ritual has been described as ‘specific signal patterns’. Dogs, coyotes, and wolves all display the same behavior. It begins with the bowing of heads – the ‘play bow’ – and, like other signals, seems to send the message that they want to play. Indeed, it serves another important function, too; it **27) …** playtime. This might seem bizarre, but it prevents any confusion in other contexts, such as mating or aggressive encounters. The psychologist, Marc Bekoff, observed that the bow is used both before and after actions that could be misunderstood as non-playful. It appears that all age groups of dogs use the play bow 74% of the time before and after pretend biting; young wolves 79% of the time and juvenile coyotes 92%.

One theory is that the playful interaction between dogs might possibly be a form of practice for any future aggressive or hunting encounters. Their playtime perhaps enables dogs to be more psychologically flexible, rather than aggressive from the moment they see a dog they have not previously met. Perhaps their playing serves no adaptive or **28) …** function, but children also play just to have a good time. Children, though, also often play games that have a more serious purpose, as they enable them to understand their place in the social hierarchy in which they live. It is not outside the realms of possibility that the same principle applies to animals and birds. Perhaps they are just exploring ways of finding their niche in animal society.

Similarly, primates such as chimpanzees and orangutans exhibit complex forms of play that provide further insight into the cognitive and social aspects of animal behavior. Young chimpanzees often engage in rough-and-tumble play, chasing, wrestling, and somersaulting with their peers. This type of play not only strengthens social bonds but also helps develop motor skills and coordination. Interestingly, researchers have observed chimpanzees creating **29) …** toys from sticks and leaves, carrying them around and treating them with care, much like children do with their favorite dolls or action figures. This behavior suggests a level of imaginative play, indicating that the capacity for such activities is not uniquely human. These observations highlight the broader spectrum of play across species, reinforcing the idea that play is a fundamental, **30) …** part of life in the animal kingdom.','published',6,'2026-09-05T09:11:25.183Z','2026-09-08T17:55:17.215Z','2026-09-05T09:11:25.183Z','poster','[{"revealAnswers":false,"completionText":"","completionMaterials":[],"id":"final-stage-1788635513199","type":"final-stage","title":"Questions 26–40","instructions":"Read the text and answer questions 26–40 below.","wordFormation":[{"number":26,"baseWord":"long","answers":["repetitive"]},{"number":27,"baseWord":"rich","answers":["prolongs","lengthens"]},{"number":28,"baseWord":"evolve","answers":["evolutionary"]},{"number":29,"baseWord":"repeat","answers":["rudimentary"]},{"number":30,"baseWord":"rudiment","answers":["enriching"]}],"trueFalse":[{"number":31,"prompt":"Young chimpanzees frequently engage in physical play with their parents.","answers":["B"]},{"number":32,"prompt":"Some age groups of dogs use the play bow before and after pretend biting.","answers":["A","B"]},{"number":33,"prompt":"Researchers discovered that younger herring gulls were more inclined to participate in the dropping game compared to their weaker counterparts.","answers":["C"]},{"number":34,"prompt":"Canines’ diet primarily consists of shellfish, which necessitate being opened prior to consumption.","answers":["B"]},{"number":35,"prompt":"Play is the lifeblood of the Animalia.","answers":["A"]}],"multipleChoice":[{"number":36,"prompt":"Why does the writer give the example of their children in the park?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To express their wonder at bird behaviour."},{"label":"B","text":"To help readers visualize what they are describing."},{"label":"C","text":"To emphasise how playful bird are."},{"label":"D","text":"To show how birds imitate humans."}],"answers":["B"]},{"number":37,"prompt":"According to the writer, herring gulls","options":[{"label":"A","text":"act in a way that is not exclusive to their breed."},{"label":"B","text":"show a natural instinct to stay alive in the wild."},{"label":"C","text":"throw clam shells to the ground from a great height."},{"label":"D","text":"fail to eat any of the clams because the shells are unbreakable."}],"answers":["A"]},{"number":38,"prompt":"What has the researchers’ work revealed about herring gulls?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"They prefer to play games when the weather is calm."},{"label":"B","text":"They have turned cracking clam shells into a game."},{"label":"C","text":"They discovered the older gulls rarely joined in."},{"label":"D","text":"They still do not know the exact motive behind their behaviour."}],"answers":["D"]},{"number":39,"prompt":"The writer says that when one dog encounters another,","options":[{"label":"A","text":"they imitate each other’s movements."},{"label":"B","text":"they continue walking together."},{"label":"C","text":"they start to fight with each other."},{"label":"D","text":"they behave in a fixed and expected way."}],"answers":["D"]},{"number":40,"prompt":"What is the author’s attitude towards animals and birds playing?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He considers it plausible that through play they find their place in their community."},{"label":"B","text":"He is convinced that there is a real reason for their behaviour."},{"label":"C","text":"He is uncertain how it contributes to research into why children play."},{"label":"D","text":"He doubts whether enough animal behaviour has been studied."}],"answers":["A"]}]}]','0e3461f8-d244-4934-a3db-b91191b46e4d.png','','final','Reading','[]',NULL);
INSERT INTO "articles" ("id","slug","title","dek","author","body","status","read_time","created_at","updated_at","published_at","layout_style","tasks_json","header_image_key","header_image_alt","stage","topic","attachments_json","subgroup") VALUES (651,'civilisation','Civilisation','','Ian','**CIVILISATION**

Civilisation can be interpreted in many ways and has meant different things to different people: to Levi-Strauss its (0) … (relating to something’s basic or most important qualities) feature was boiled food; Nietzsche wanted to (1) … (to change the direction, order, position, result, etc. of something to its opposite) it and Arnold Toynbee called it ‘progress towards sainthood’.
We all use the word loosely to mean culture we approve of. Yet it is not too late to (2) … (to help something out of a dangerous or unpleasant situation) civilisation from misrepresentation. An old and disinterested (3) … (an idea or mental image of something) of what it means to be civilised is waiting to be revived.
At the heart of every civilising project there is a common (4) … (something that is the force behind or reason for something else): this is the human itch to transform nature, to mould earth, cleave waves and (5) … (to bend, twist, or change something from its natural shape or condition) environments in improbable ways. Civilisations are actively engaged in reshaping the world for human use, stamping landscapes with new (6) … (new organised layouts or arrangements on the land) of clearings and channels, field systems and street grids. In exceptional cases, civilisations try to (7) … (to separate from or break away from something) from nature altogether, to deny the animal side of humanity and to domesticate the wild man within by elaborate manners.','published',5,'2026-09-05T15:27:54.425Z','2026-09-06T17:07:05.488Z','2026-09-05T15:27:54.425Z','essay','[{"matchingMode":"dropdown","matchingShowLabels":true,"matchingReuse":true,"id":"crossword-1788657728839","type":"crossword","title":"Questions 1–7","instructions":"For items 1–7, solve the crossword using the definitions in brackets. Type your answers into the numbered blanks. The first example (0) is done for you.","audioKey":null,"audioInstructions":"Now listen to a talk on the same topic and answer the questions, comparing the text and the talk. You will hear the text TWICE.","readingMinutes":10,"questions":[{"number":0,"prompt":"CIVILISATION\nCivilisation can be interpreted in many ways and has meant different things to different people: to Levi-Strauss its (0) … (relating to something’s basic or most important qualities) feature was boiled food;","answers":["essential"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":true,"row":4,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"number":1,"prompt":"Nietzsche wanted to **(1) …** (to change the direction, order, position, result, etc. of something to its opposite) it and Arnold Toynbee called it ‘progress towards sainthood’.","answers":["reverse"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":1,"col":3,"direction":"down","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[2,5]},{"number":2,"prompt":"We all use the word loosely to mean culture we approve of. Yet it is not too late to **(2) …** (to help something out of a dangerous or unpleasant situation) civilisation from misrepresentation.","answers":["rescue"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":7,"col":2,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[5,0]},{"number":3,"prompt":"An old and disinterested (3) … (an idea or mental image of something) of what it means to be civilised is waiting to be revived.","answers":["vision"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":5,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[2]},{"number":4,"prompt":"At the heart of every civilising project there is a common **(4) …** (something that is the force behind or reason for something else):","answers":["impulse"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":8,"direction":"down","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[4]},{"number":5,"prompt":"this is the human itch to transform nature, to mould earth, cleave waves and **(5) …** (to bend, twist, or change something from its natural shape or condition) environments in improbable ways.","answers":["warp"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":2,"col":5,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[2]},{"number":6,"prompt":"Civilisations are actively engaged in reshaping the world for human use, stamping landscapes with new **(6) …** (organised layouts or arrangements on the land) of clearings and channels, field systems and street grids.","answers":["patterns"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"down","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[3,2]},{"number":7,"prompt":"In exceptional cases, civilisations try to **(7) …** (to separate from or break away from something) from nature altogether, to deny the animal side of humanity and to domesticate the wild man within by elaborate manners.","answers":["secede"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":5,"col":8,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]}],"columns":[{"title":"Descriptions","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""},{"label":"G","text":""},{"label":"H","text":""}]}]}]',NULL,'','regional','Use of English','[]','Crossword');
