INSERT INTO "articles" ("id","slug","title","dek","author","body","status","read_time","created_at","updated_at","published_at","layout_style","tasks_json","header_image_key","header_image_alt","stage","topic","attachments_json","subgroup") VALUES (678,'charting-the-world','Charting the world','','Ian','**CHARTING THE WORLD**

British (0) … (people who travel to unfamiliar, uncharted, or little-known regions to discover, map, or study them) have greatly shaped modern knowledge of world geography. In the sixteenth century, Sir Francis Drake became the first English sailor to (1) … (sail completely round) the globe, proving that global sea routes could connect distant lands. Later, Captain James Cook carried out important (2) … (long journeys, especially by sea or in space) across the Pacific Ocean, mapping Australia, New Zealand, and many islands with impressive accuracy. These journeys improved (3) … (the activity or skill of planning and directing the course of a ship, aircraft, or other vehicle), created new trade opportunities, and placed Britain at the center of (4) … (connected with human activity at sea) exploration.

Exploration also continued on land and in extreme environments. In the nineteenth century, David Livingstone traveled through central Africa, recording rivers, lakes, and (5) … (large areas of land that can be seen in one view) unknown to Europeans. At the same time, polar explorers such as Robert Falcon Scott and Ernest Shackleton pushed into the harsh climate of Antarctica to study its geography and nature. Through their (6) … (the quality of trying to do something even when it is difficult) and curiosity, British explorers (7) … (made larger or more detailed) the world map and deepened scientific understanding of the planet.','published',5,'2026-09-06T11:54:22.152Z','2026-09-06T11:55:28.964Z','2026-09-06T11:54:22.152Z','essay','[{"id":"crossword-1788731529297","type":"crossword","title":"Questions 1–7","instructions":"For items 1–7, solve the crossword using the definitions in brackets. Type your answers into the numbered blanks. The first example (0) is done for you.","audioKey":null,"audioInstructions":"Now listen to a talk on the same topic and then do the tasks (questions 16-25), comparing the text above and the talk. You will hear the text TWICE.","readingMinutes":10,"questions":[{"number":0,"prompt":"","answers":["explorers"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":true,"row":0,"col":13,"direction":"down","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"number":1,"prompt":"","answers":["circumnavigate"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":6,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[2,12,11,8]},{"number":2,"prompt":"","answers":["voyages"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":9,"col":6,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[2]},{"number":3,"prompt":"","answers":["navigation"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":1,"col":7,"direction":"down","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[4,0]},{"number":4,"prompt":"","answers":["maritime"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":5,"direction":"down","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[4,0]},{"number":5,"prompt":"","answers":["landscapes"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":1,"col":3,"direction":"down","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[5,4]},{"number":6,"prompt":"","answers":["determination"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":1,"direction":"down","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[1,2,12]},{"number":7,"prompt":"","answers":["expanded"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":3,"col":11,"direction":"down","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[1]}],"columns":[{"title":"Descriptions","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""},{"label":"G","text":""},{"label":"H","text":""}]}]}]',NULL,'','regional','Use of English','[]','Crossword');
INSERT INTO "articles" ("id","slug","title","dek","author","body","status","read_time","created_at","updated_at","published_at","layout_style","tasks_json","header_image_key","header_image_alt","stage","topic","attachments_json","subgroup") VALUES (679,'us-activists','US Activists','','Ian','','published',5,'2026-09-06T11:58:26.933Z','2026-09-06T14:44:00.384Z','2026-09-06T11:58:26.933Z','essay','[{"matchingMode":"interactive","matchingShowLabels":true,"matchingReuse":false,"id":"column-match-1788731775943","type":"column-match","title":"Questions 1–6","instructions":"For items 1–6, match each item in the left column with a description. There is an extra description that you will not need.","audioKey":null,"audioInstructions":"Now listen to a talk on the same topic and then do the tasks (questions 16-25), comparing the text above and the talk. You will hear the text TWICE.","readingMinutes":10,"questions":[{"number":1,"prompt":"Harriet Tubman","answers":["F"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"number":2,"prompt":"Malcolm X","answers":["E"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":2,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"number":3,"prompt":"Rosa Parks","answers":["B"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":4,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"number":4,"prompt":"Martin Luther King Jr.","answers":["G"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":6,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"number":5,"prompt":"Frederick Douglass","answers":["C"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":8,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"number":6,"prompt":"William Lloyd Garrison","answers":["A"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":10,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]}],"columns":[{"title":"Descriptions","options":[{"label":"A","text":"He was a white American abolitionist, journalist, and reformer. He strongly opposed slavery and believed it should be ended immediately and completely. He published an influential anti-slavery newspaper called *The Liberator* and helped found the American Anti-Slavery Society. He fought for freedom and equal rights through writing, speeches, and activism. He is known as one of the most important leaders of the abolitionist movement."},{"label":"B","text":"She was an African American civil rights activist known as “the mother of the Civil Rights Movement.” In 1955, she refused to give up her seat on a segregated bus to a white passenger in Montgomery, Alabama. Her arrest started the Montgomery Bus Boycott, which became a major turning point in the fight against segregation. She is remembered for her quiet strength and courage in standing up for equal rights."},{"label":"C","text":"He was an American abolitionist, writer, and former enslaved man. After escaping slavery, he became a famous speaker and author, telling the world about the horrors of slavery through his autobiography. He fought for the end of slavery, for equal rights for Black Americans, and later for women’s rights as well. He is known as one of the greatest American voices for freedom and justice."},{"label":"D","text":"She was an African American abolitionist and women’s rights activist. She was born into slavery but escaped to freedom and dedicated her life to fighting against slavery and discrimination. She became famous for her powerful speeches, especially her 1851 speech “Ain’t I a Woman?”, in which she defended the rights of both Black people and women. She traveled across the United States speaking about equality, justice, and human dignity. She is known as a strong voice for freedom, racial equality, and women’s rights."},{"label":"E","text":"He was a powerful African American civil rights activist and speaker. He fought for Black pride, self-respect, and equality, especially for African Americans facing racism and injustice. At first, he supported separation from white society through the Nation of Islam, but later he promoted unity and human rights for all races. He was known for his strong, direct speeches and his belief that Black people should defend themselves and control their own future."},{"label":"F","text":"She was an American abolitionist and former enslaved woman who became one of the bravest leaders of the Underground Railroad. After escaping from slavery herself, she returned to the South many times to help other enslaved people reach freedom. She risked her life repeatedly and was never caught. During the Civil War, she also worked as a nurse, cook, and spy for the Union Army. She is known as a symbol of courage, freedom, and resistance against slavery."},{"label":"G","text":"He was the most famous leader of the American Civil Rights Movement. He fought against racial segregation and discrimination using peaceful protests and nonviolent resistance. He organized major marches and gave inspiring speeches, including the famous “I Have a Dream” speech. His work helped lead to important laws giving equal rights to Black Americans. He is known as a global symbol of peace, justice, and equality."}]}]}]',NULL,'','regional','Use of English','[]','Country studies');
