o Australia, the percentage who settled in Sydney and Melbourne was","answers":["C"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[{"label":"A","text":"40%."},{"label":"B","text":"50%."},{"label":"C","text":"60%."}],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":14,"prompt":"The government is considering encouraging migrants to settle in other areas by","answers":["A"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[{"label":"A","text":"offering tax incentive to employers."},{"label":"B","text":"making it easier for farmers to migrate."},{"label":"C","text":"employing people outside Sydney and Melbourne."}],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":15,"prompt":"The number of migrants living in Sydney is likely to be","answers":["A"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[{"label":"A","text":"higher than the statistics indicate."},{"label":"B","text":"lower than the statistics indicate."},{"label":"C","text":"the same as the statistics indicate."}],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]}],"columns":[{"title":"Descriptions","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""},{"label":"G","text":""},{"label":"H","text":""}]}]}]',NULL,'','regional','Listening','[]','Multiple choice');
INSERT INTO "articles" ("id","slug","title","dek","author","body","status","read_time","created_at","updated_at","published_at","layout_style","tasks_json","header_image_key","header_image_alt","stage","topic","attachments_json","subgroup") VALUES (715,'humans-are-infovores','Humans Are Infovores','','Ian','Why are you reading this when you could be watching paint dry instead? It’s all because of our innate hunger for information. Humans, it turns out, are infovores.

The term was introduced into the scientific lexicon around 2006 by neuroscientists trying to work out why we get a kick out of learning something new – why we have an appetite for knowledge. Irving Biederman of the University of Southern California in University Park and Edward Vessel of New York University, who coined the term, now think they have identified the brain mechanism responsible. They claim that the neural pathways through which we learn about the world tap into the same pleasure networks in the brain that are activated by drugs like heroin. They say that, for humans, only the basic urges of hunger, harm avoidance and the need to find a mate can distract us from this information craving.

How does information give people a high? The key is a type of chemical receptor known as a mu-opioid receptor, which is found on the surface of some brain cells. Like other opioid receptors, it is activated by heroin, morphine or naturally produced substances called endorphins, and is found in areas of the brain that mediate pain and pleasure. Mu-opioid receptors are also found in areas that process sensory information and memories. They occur in increasing numbers along the neural pathways in these areas, from the early stages, where the brain processes basic things like colour, to the later stages of conscious recognition.

These are the areas that become active when the brain is trying to interpret the information it is receiving, whether that is an image of an object, words on a page or the song of a bird. Biederman and Vessel suggest that when this happens, the endorphins that stimulate mu-opioid receptors are released, causing a feeling of pleasure. What’s more, because the number of mu-opioid receptors increases further along the neural processing pathway, information that triggers the most memories and conveys the most meaning to a person causes the greatest pleasure response. It is this bonus that compels people to browse for new information.

Does the effect ever wear thin? Yes, with repetition. Reading a book for the second time is less stimulating than reading it for the first time. That is, unless you didn’t understand it the first time round. Biederman and Vessel say that endorphins are released at the “click” of comprehension and that, until the penny drops, people are happy to return to a subject. Children take longer to “click” than adults, which explains their enthusiasm for hearing the same bedtime story night after night.','published',5,'2026-09-08T17:05:58.113Z','2026-09-08T17:05:58.113Z','2026-09-08T17:05:58.113Z','essay','[{"revealAnswers":false,"completionText":"","completionMaterials":[],"readingPrompt":"Now you have {minutes} minutes to read the text.","showReadingTimer":true,"matchingMode":"dropdown","matchingShowLabels":true,"matchingReuse":true,"id":"listening-integrated-1788922881807","type":"listening-integrated","title":"Questions 16–25","instructions":"Read the text below, then listen to a talk on the same topic. You will notice that some ideas coincide and some differ in them. Answer questions 16-25 by choosing A if the idea is expressed in both materials, B if it can be found only in the reading text, C if it can be found only in the audio-recording, and D if neither of the materials expresses the idea.","audioKey":"3547906b-9836-4ee7-bc51-3dd876c675e5.mp3","audioInstructions":"Now listen to a talk on the same topic and then do the tasks (questions 16-25), comparing the text above and the talk. You will hear the text TWICE.","readingMinutes":10,"questions":[{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":16,"prompt":"Humans are naturally driven to seek out new knowledge.","answers":["A"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":17,"prompt":"Social media algorithms exploit biological reward mechanisms in the brain.","answers":["D"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":18,"prompt":"Modern digital platforms intentionally design content to maintain users’ attention.","answers":["C"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":19,"prompt":"Two neuroscientists came up with the term.","answers":["B"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":20,"prompt":"The pleasure derived from learning is linked to the release of specific natural chemicals in the brain.","answers":["B"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":21,"prompt":"Children pose interest in stories because their brains process information faster than adults’.","answers":["D"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":22,"prompt":"People frequently interrupt everyday activities to check for new information.","answers":["C"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":23,"prompt":"Today’s media environment makes it hard to disconnect from constant updates.","answers":["C"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":24,"prompt":"Fundamental survival instincts can outweigh the desire to acquire information.","answers":["A"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":25,"prompt":"People return to previously studied material only when they failed to understand it initially.","answers":["D"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]}],"columns":[{"title":"Descriptions","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""},{"label":"G","text":""},{"label":"H","text":""}]}]}]',NULL,'','final','Listening','[]','Integrated L&R');
INSERT INTO "articles" ("id","slug","title","dek","author","body","status","read_time","created_at","updated_at","published_at","layout_style","tasks_json","header_image_key","header_image_alt","stage","topic","attachments_json","subgroup") VALUES (716,'analogies','Analogies','','Ian','','published',5,'2026-09-08T17:28:37.292Z','2026-09-08T17:28:37.292Z','2026-09-08T17:28:37.292Z','essay','[{"revealAnswers":false,"completionText":"","completionMaterials":[],"readingPrompt":"Now you have {minutes} minutes to read the text.","showReadingTimer":true,"matchingMode":"dropdown","matchingShowLabels":true,"matchingReuse":true,"id":"gap-fill-1788924310558","type":"gap-fill","title":"Questions 1–7","instructions":"For items 1–10, complete the sentences with one word to make analogies. The number is letters is specified.","audioKey":null,"audioInstructions":"Now listen to a talk on the same topic and then do the tasks (questions 16-25), comparing the text above and the talk. You will hear the text TWICE.","readingMinutes":10,"questions":[{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":1,"prompt":"Dream is to sleep as _____ is to hayfever. (6 letters)","answers":["sneeze"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":2,"prompt":"Sharp is to dull as bent is to _____. (8 letters)","answers":["straight"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":3,"prompt":"Glacier is to ice as volcano is to _____. (4 letters)","answers":["lava"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":4,"prompt":"Blueprint is to building as recipe is to _____. (4 letters)","answers":["dish","meal"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":5,"prompt":"Sap is to tree as blood is to _____. (4 letters)","answers":["vein","body"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":6,"prompt":"Architect is to building as choreographer is to _____. (5 letters)","answers":["dance"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":7,"prompt":"Apprentice is to master as cadet is to _____. (7 letters)","answers":["officer"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":8,"prompt":"Syllabus is to course as script is to _____. (4 letters)","answers":["play"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":9,"prompt":"Thread is to needle as fuel is to _____. (6 letters)","answers":["engine"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]},{"additionalBlanks":[],"number":10,"prompt":"Pebble is to boulder as brook is to _____. (5 letters)","answers":["river"],"extraAnswers":[],"options":[],"example":false,"row":0,"col":0,"direction":"across","imageKey":null,"imageAlt":"","givenLetters":[]}],"columns":[{"title":"Descriptions","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""},{"label":"G","text":""},{"label":"H","text":""}]}]}]',NULL,'','final','Use of English','[]','Vocabulary');
INSERT INTO "articles" ("id","slug","title","dek","author","body","status","read_time","created_at","updated_at","published_at","layout_style","tasks_json","header_image_key","header_image_alt","stage","topic","attachments_json","subgroup") VALUES (717,'living-in-a-dream-world','Living in a Dream World','Daydreaming can help solve problems, trigger creativity, and inspire great works of art and science. When it becomes compulsive, however, the consequences can be dire.','Ian','Most people spend about 30 per cent of their waking hours spacing out, drifting off, getting lost in thought, wool-gathering, or building castles in the air. Yale University emeritus psychology professor Jerome L. Singer defines daydreaming as shifting attention ‘away from some primary physical or mental task toward an unfolding sequence of private responses’ or, more simply, ‘watching your own mental videos’. He also divides daydreaming styles into two main categories: ‘positive-constructive’, which includes upbeat and imaginative thoughts, and ‘dysphoric’, which encompasses visions of failure or punishment.

Other scientists distinguish between mundane musings and extravagant fantasies. Michael Kane, a cognitive psychologist at the University of North Carolina, considers ‘mind-wandering’ to be ‘any thoughts that are unrelated to one’s task at hand’. In his view, mind-wandering is a broad category that may include everything from pondering ingredients for a dinner recipe to saving the planet from an alien invasion. Most of the time, when people’s minds wander, they are thinking about everyday concerns such as recent encounters and items on their to-do lists.

Such humdrum concerns figured prominently in one study that **26) …** measured how much time we spend mind-wandering in daily life. In a 2009 study, Kane and his colleague Jennifer McVay asked 72 students to carry Palm Pilots that beeped at random eight times a day for a week. The subjects then recorded their thoughts at that moment on a questionnaire. The study found that about 30 per cent of the beeps coincided with thoughts unrelated to the task at hand, and that mind-wandering increased with stress, boredom, or sleepiness and decreased during enjoyable tasks. That may be because certain activities tend to grab our attention.

However, intense focus on our problems may not always lead to immediate solutions. Instead, allowing the mind to float freely can enable us to access unconscious ideas hovering beneath the surface – a process that can lead to creative insight, according to psychologist Jonathan Schooler of the University of California, Santa Barbara.

We may not even be aware that we are daydreaming. We have all had the experience of ‘reading’ a book yet absorbing nothing – moving our eyes over the words on a page as our attention wanders and the text turns into gibberish. ‘When this happens, people lack what I call “meta-awareness”, consciousness of what is going on in their minds,’ he says. But aimless mental ramblings can be productive, allowing us to stumble upon ideas and associations that we may never find if we **27) …** seek them.

Artists and scientists are well acquainted with such playful fantasising. Filmmaker Tim Burton dreamed his way to Hollywood success, spending his childhood holed up in his bedroom, creating posters for an imaginary horror film series. Novelist Orhan Pamuk imagined ‘another world’ to which he retreated as a child. Albert Einstein pictured himself running along a light wave – a reverie that led to his theory of special relativity.

So, why should daydreaming aid creativity? It may be partly because, when the brain is floating in **28) …** mental space, it serves a specific purpose. It allows us to engage in one task and, at the same time, trigger reminders of other, concurrent goals so that we do not lose sight of them. There is also the belief that we can boost the creative process by increasing the amount of daydreaming we do or by replaying variants of the millions of events we store in our brains.

Yet to enhance creativity, it is important to pay attention to daydreams. Schooler calls this ‘tuning out’, or deliberate ‘off-task thinking’: the ability not only to let the mind wander but also to remain aware of that wandering. Those who are most creative also need the meta-awareness to realise when a creative idea has popped into their minds.

The mind’s freedom to wander during deliberate tuning out could also explain the flash of insight that may coincide with taking a break from an unsolved problem. A study conducted at the University of Lancaster into this possibility found that, if we allow our minds to ramble during a moderately challenging task, we can access ideas that are not easily available to our conscious minds. Our ability to do so is now known to depend on a dedicated daydreaming network deep in our brains.

Like Facebook for the brain, the default network is a bustling web of memories and streaming movies starring ourselves. ‘When we daydream, we’re at the centre of our universe,’ says neurologist Marcus Raichle of Washington University, who first described the network in 2001. It consists of three main regions that help us imagine ourselves and the thoughts and feelings of others, draw personal memories from the brain, and access episodic memories.

It was not until 2007, however, that cognitive psychologist Malia Fox Mason discovered that the default network – which lights up when people switch from an attention-demanding activity to drifting reveries with no specific goals – becomes more active when mind-wandering is more likely. She also discovered that people who daydream more in everyday life show greater **29) …** in the default network while performing monotonous tasks.

Although these two findings were significant, mind-wandering itself was not measured during the scans. As a result, it could not be determined exactly when the participants in her study were ‘on task’ and when they were daydreaming. In 2009, Schooler, together with his colleagues Jonathan Smallwood and Kalina Christoff of the University of British Columbia, published the first study to link mental wandering with increased activity in the default network. Scans of the participants in their study revealed that activity in the default network was strongest when the subjects were unaware that they had lost focus.

The conclusion reached in this ground-breaking study was that the more complex a mind-wandering episode is, the more of our mental resources it is likely to consume. This inevitably leads to the problem of determining the point at which creative daydreaming crosses the boundary into the realm of compulsive fantasising. Although there is often a fine dividing line between the two, one question that can help resolve the dilemma is whether the benefits gained from daydreaming outweigh the costs to the daydreamer’s reputation and performance.

According to Schooler, there are two steps you need to take to make the distinction. First, notice whether you are deriving any useful insights from your fantasies. Second, take stock of the content of your daydreams. To distinguish between beneficial and pathological imaginings, he adds, ‘Ask yourself if this is something useful, helpful, available, pleasant, or am I just **30) …** the same thoughts over and over again?’ And if daydreaming feels out of control, then, even if it is pleasant, it is probably not useful or valuable.

On the other hand, there are psychologists who feel that the boundary is not so easily defined. They argue that mind-wandering is not inherently good or bad, as it depends to a great extent on context. When, for example, daydreaming occurs during an activity that requires little concentration, it is unlikely to be costly. If, however, it causes someone to suffer severe injury or worse by, say, walking into traffic, then the line has been crossed.','published',5,'2026-09-08T18:20:07.787Z','2026-09-08T19:10:16.783Z','2026-09-08T18:41:56.269Z','folio','[{"revealAnswers":false,"completionText":"","completionMaterials":[],"id":"final-stage-1788926882796","type":"final-stage","title":"Questions 26–40","instructions":"Read the text and answer questions 26–40 below.","wordFormation":[{"number":26,"baseWord":"rigor","answers":["rigorously"]},{"number":27,"baseWord":"intend","answers":["intentionally"]},{"number":28,"baseWord":"focus","answers":["unfocused"]},{"number":29,"baseWord":"act","answers":["activity"]},{"number":30,"baseWord":"hash","answers":["rehashing"]}],"trueFalse":[{"number":31,"prompt":"Mind-wandering can place substantial demands on cognitive capacity.","answers":["A"]},{"number":32,"prompt":"Habitual daydreamers tend to outperform others creatively.","answers":["C"]},{"number":33,"prompt":"Keeping multiple goals mentally active inevitably undermines performance on the task at hand.","answers":["B"]},{"number":34,"prompt":"Useful ideas cannot emerge unless distraction is consciously noticed.","answers":["B"]},{"number":35,"prompt":"Frequent mind-wandering is indicative of weaker attentional control.","answers":["C"]}],"multipleChoice":[{"number":36,"prompt":"The experience of reading without absorbing anything chiefly illustrates that","options":[{"label":"A","text":"awareness of distraction tends to precede the loss of comprehension."},{"label":"B","text":"comprehension may continue despite a temporary lapse in attention."},{"label":"C","text":"passive reading is particularly conducive to productive mind-wandering."},{"label":"D","text":"attention can lapse without the individual immediately registering it."}],"answers":["D"]},{"number":37,"prompt":"","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""}],"answers":[]},{"number":38,"prompt":"Why might disengaging from a problem prove productive?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It reduces the complexity of the problem"},{"label":"B","text":"It allows latent associations to surface."},{"label":"C","text":"It strengthens deliberate recall."},{"label":"D","text":"It prevents competing goals from intruding."}],"answers":[]},{"number":39,"prompt":"","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""}],"answers":[]},{"number":40,"prompt":"The writer’s attitude towards daydreaming throughout the article is best described as","options":[{"label":"A","text":"circumspectly favourable."},{"label":"B","text":"sanctimonious."},{"label":"C","text":"faintly disparaging."},{"label":"D","text":"unreservedly laudatory."}],"answers":["A"]}]}]','1a17f861-5724-4969-aebf-854a58dfc24a.png','','final','Reading','[]',NULL);
